## Registrations per frame (`--features probe-sites`, v4k_8tile, counts only)

| t | base | head |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 7,924,706 (68 sites) | 7,924,706 (68 sites) |
| 8 | 22,700,725 (68 sites) | 7,923,518 (67 sites) |

`lost=0` on all four. Reproduces #469's counts exactly.

**ns/registration, derived from my own two measurements:**

- t=8: 14.78 M registrations removed bought 14.8 ms/frame -> **1.00 ns per
  removed registration** (marginal cost).
- t=1: **zero** registrations removed and wall rose 5.6 ms/frame -> the
  surviving registrations got **+0.71 ns each**. That is the row-map division
  plus the two column stores, paid by a path that removes nothing.

This is the campaign's "count is not cost" rule in both directions at once: the
win is entirely where the count moved, and the regression is entirely where it
did not.

## Corpus

`examples/md5_inventory` over the full `dav1d-test-data` corpus on the head:
768 rows, **766 PASS / 0 mismatch / 0 error / 2 SKIP**.

Set-diff BY NAME (key `group/name`, value `(status, actual_md5)`) against the
committed baseline `benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst`:
only-in-baseline **0**, only-in-head **0**, differing **0**.

**But the gate is single-threaded.** `md5_inventory` uses `Settings::default()`
and `Settings::threads` defaults to 1, so `tile_threading_active()` is false for
all 768 vectors and every rectangle caller takes the branch main already took.
766/766 therefore says the rect RECORD is pixel-inert serially; it says nothing
about the concurrent path, which is the entire hazard surface of this change.

## Soundness — Miri, reproduced independently

`cargo +nightly miri test -p rav1d-disjoint-mut --test rect_hull_aliasing`,
each test run in isolation (Miri aborts on first UB):

| case | Miri |
|---|---|
| `the_hulls_of_two_accepted_rectangles_overlap` (arithmetic only) | pass |
| `the_tracker_accepts_both` (guards created, not written through) | pass |
| CONTROL `control_per_row_guards_from_two_tile_columns_do_not_alias` | pass |
| CONTROL `control_one_hull_guard_is_exclusive` | pass |
| `writing_through_both_guards_while_both_are_live` | **UB** |
| `four_tile_columns_the_way_the_decoder_holds_them` | **UB** |

`trying to retag from <N> for Unique permission ... that tag does not exist in
the borrow stack`, raised at `DisjointMutGuard::deref_mut`
(`crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/src/lib.rs:322`). Both controls passing is the
attribution: this is the rectangle guard, not `DisjointMut`. `StridedRows` and
`index_rect_mut` have 0 occurrences on ee07b00, so the hazard arrives with the
primitive.

## Oracle — reproduced to the digit

`RECT_ORACLE_ITERS=200000`: 63,020 overlapping / 1,536,980 disjoint,
**0 missed overlaps**, 2,163 conservative false positives (0.14% of disjoint
pairs), all row-crossing, 1.75 s.

Mutation teeth, each restored and verified by sha256 + `git diff --exit-code`:

| mutation | shipped `rowmap_tests` | `rect_oracle` |
|---|---|---|
| M1 `cols_meet` -> `a0 <= b0 && b0 <= a1` (misses overlaps) | **9/9 GREEN** | FAIL, 8/8 shapes `MISSED OVERLAP (UNSOUND)` |
| M2 (mine) `StridedRows::shape` claims `w-1` columns | 8/9, `adjacent_tile_columns_on_the_same_rows_do_not_conflict` FAILS | FAIL, `MISSED OVERLAP (UNSOUND)` |

M1 confirms #470's central claim: the nine shipped mechanism tests cannot
catch a predicate that hands out aliasing `&mut`. M2 shows they are not
entirely toothless — a width mutation is caught.

Oracle coverage gap I did NOT see named: `conflicts()` and `hold()` both take
**mutable** borrows only, so `index_rect` / `rect_as` — the IMMUTABLE rectangle
path, which uses a different scan mask (`occupied & self.mutable`) and is the
path that panicked in CI — is never exercised. The oracle is also
single-threaded.

