## CI — six jobs green on `main` are red on this head, and one red one is a race

`main` @ ee07b00 check-runs: Clippy, Lint, Documentation, Build & Test
(macos-15-intel / ubuntu-latest i686 / windows-latest i686) all **success**.
Four `disjoint-mut CI` Test legs (`--no-default-features` x3, `--all-features`)
and `Miri (Object)` are pre-existing failures on main and stay failures here.

On `perf/strided-rect` @ 4db4144 the six above are **fail**:

| job | failure |
|---|---|
| Build & Test (ubuntu-latest, i686) | `error: this arithmetic operation will overflow` at `tracker_shard.rs:1294:61` |
| Build & Test (windows-latest, i686) | same |
| Clippy / Lint | `manual !RangeInclusive::contains` (1294:12) + `casting to the same type` (1316:38), `-D warnings` |
| Documentation | 3 unresolved intra-doc links: `Self::mut_slice_as`, `Bounds::row_w`, `Bounds::rows` |
| Build & Test (macos-15-intel) | `overlapping DisjointMut` panic in `rav1d-worker-6` -> decode failure |

### The 32-bit break is wider than the two red jobs say

`1usize << 32` at `tracker_shard.rs:1294` overflows wherever `usize` is 32 bits.
Reproduced locally: `cargo build --release --target wasm32-wasip1` FAILS on this
head and SUCCEEDS on ee07b00. **CI does not see it** — the `Cross-compile
(wasm32-wasip1)` job runs `cargo check`, which stops before the MIR pass that
raises `arithmetic_overflow`, so it is green on a target that cannot build.
Third gate in this campaign that is green for a structural reason.

### The macOS-Intel decode failure is intermittent, which is worse

```
thread 'rav1d-worker-6' panicked at include/dav1d/picture.rs:568:19:
  overlapping DisjointMut:
   current:  & _[16625..32769] on ThreadId(25) at include/dav1d/picture.rs:568:19
  existing: &mut _[32768..36624]
     0: overlap_panic at crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/src/tracker_shard.rs:1678
    14: rav1d_filter_sbrow_deblock_cols at src/recon.rs:3719
    15: rav1d_worker_task
```

`picture.rs:568` is the new `Rav1dPictureDataComponent::rect` (immutable
rectangle). The failing test is `decode_md5_committed::lr_sgr_vectors_threaded_
match_reference_md5` — 8 workers, committed LR/SGR vectors — and it fails as a
DECODE ERROR, not a wrong md5. Green on ee07b00 (2 runs) and on PR #468.

It **passed on re-run** of the same job (7m12s), so it is a race, not a
deterministic x86 break: 1 failure in 2 macos-15-intel runs. Not reproduced on
aarch64 in 1,600 local runs (40 sequential + 1,560 at 24x oversubscription) nor
in the 12-decoder pressure run. An intermittent `overlapping DisjointMut` is a
worse failure mode than a deterministic one — it merges green.

Note the hull path this now takes under tile threading is the one
`with_pixel_guard_immut`'s own surviving doc comment calls unsafe: "wide guards
(`strided_slice_mut` / `narrow_guard`) are unsafe under tile threading even in
the immutable case (they cover `(h-1)*stride + w` bytes and overlap with
adjacent tiles' mutable ranges)". The change narrows the RECORD and leaves that
sentence — and the hull reference — in place.

